In C#, pattern matching provides a powerful way to work with data based on its shape or structure. If you're looking to extract a currency name from a string with the format "balance_currency", where "currency" is the part you're interested in, there are several ways to achieve this, including using regular expressions, string manipulation methods like `Split`, or pattern matching introduced in C# 7 and enhanced in later versions. 

### Using Regular Expressions

If the currency format is consistent and you're looking for a specific pattern, regular expressions can be a very effective tool. Here's how you could use them:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string input = "balance_USD";
        string pattern = @"balance_(\w+)";
        var match = Regex.Match(input, pattern);

        if (match.Success)
        {
            string currency = match.Groups[1].Value;
            Console.WriteLine(currency);  // Outputs: USD
        }
    }
}
```

### Using String Split

Another approach is to use the `Split` method if the structure is as straightforward as "balance_currency":

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string input = "balance_USD";
        string[] parts = input.Split('_');

        if (parts.Length > 1)
        {
            string currency = parts[1];
            Console.WriteLine(currency);  // Outputs: USD
        }
    }
}
```

### Using Pattern Matching

For the given problem, pattern matching directly for strings isn't as straightforward because pattern matching shines in scenarios where you're dealing with different types or shapes of data. However, for completeness, consider a scenario where you're matching types and extracting information based on the type:

```csharp
object input = "balance_USD";

if (input is string str && str.StartsWith("balance_"))
{
    string currency = str.Split('_')[1];
    Console.WriteLine(currency);  // Outputs: USD
}
```

While this still uses `Split`, it demonstrates how you could incorporate pattern matching into the process, especially if dealing with various types.

### Conclusion

The method you choose depends on the specifics of your scenario including the format of your input string, performance considerations, and code readability. Regular expressions are very powerful and suitable for more complex patterns, while string methods like `Split` offer simplicity and readability for straightforward cases. Pattern matching is incredibly useful for more complex type-related patterns rather than simple string operations.

To ensure the `main` element takes up the remainder of the viewport height not occupied by the `header`, you can use Flexbox or CSS Grid layouts in combination with viewport units or the `calc()` function. Both of these approaches enable you to dynamically allocate the remaining space to the `main` element regardless of the `header` element's height.

Here's an outline of how you can achieve this with both methods:

### Using Flexbox

1. Set the `body` element to display as a flex container in a column direction.
2. Ensure the `header` element takes up only as much space as it needs.
3. Allow the `main` element to grow and fill the remaining space.

#### HTML Structure

```html
<body>
    <header>
        <!-- Your header content here -->
    </header>
    <main>
        <!-- Your main content here -->
    </main>
</body>
```

#### CSS (Flexbox Approach)

```css
html, body {
    height: 100%; /* Make sure the body can stretch to the full viewport height */
    margin: 0; /* Remove default margin */
}

body {
    display: flex; /* Establish a flex container */
    flex-direction: column; /* Stack elements vertically */
}

header {
    /* Header will take up as much space as it needs */
}

main {
    flex-grow: 1; /* Allow main to take up the remaining space */
    overflow: auto; /* Add scrollbar to main if content overflows */
}
```

This method ensures that the `main` part of your page dynamically adjusts its size to fill the space not occupied by the `header`.

### Using CSS Grid

Alternatively, you can use CSS Grid to achieve a similar effect with potentially more control over layout grid areas if your design complexity increases.

#### CSS (CSS Grid Approach)

```css
html, body {
    height: 100%; /* Ensure the body can stretch to the full viewport height */
    margin: 0; /* Remove default margin */
}

body {
    display: grid; /* Establish a grid container */
    grid-template-rows: auto 1fr; /* First row for header (auto-sized), second row for main (fill remaining) */
}

header {
    /* Header will take up as much space as it needs */
}

main {
    overflow: auto; /* Add scrollbar to main if content overflows */
}
```

Both of these methods are clean and effectively utilize modern CSS features to ensure your `main` element dynamically occupies the remaining space left by the `header`. The choice between Flexbox and Grid can be based on personal preference or specific layout requirements, as both can handle this task well.

Here's a sample C++ program that can detect whenever a DLL is being injected into a process using the Windows API.

```cpp
#include <windows.h>
#include <iostream>

// Function to check if a DLL is being injected
bool IsDLLInjected()
{
    // Enumerate loaded modules
    HMODULE hModules[1024];
    DWORD cbNeeded;
    if (!EnumProcessModules(GetCurrentProcess(), hModules, sizeof(hModules), &cbNeeded))
    {
        return false;
    }

    // Get the name of each module
    TCHAR moduleName[MAX_PATH];
    for (DWORD i = 0; i < (cbNeeded / sizeof(HMODULE)); i++)
    {
        if (GetModuleFileNameEx(GetCurrentProcess(), hModules[i], moduleName, sizeof(moduleName) / sizeof(TCHAR)))
        {
            std::wstring moduleStr(moduleName);

            // Check if the module name contains ".dll"
            if (moduleStr.find(L".dll") != std::wstring::npos)
            {
                return true; // DLL is being injected
            }
        }
    }

    return false; // No DLL injection detected
}

int main()
{
    if (IsDLLInjected())
    {
        std::cout << "DLL injected!" << std::endl;
    }
    else
    {
        std::cout << "No DLL injection detected." << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Note that this code uses the Windows API functions `EnumProcessModules` and `GetModuleFileNameEx` to enumerate the loaded modules in the current process and check if any of them have a ".dll" extension in their name. If a DLL is found, it indicates that a DLL is being injected into the process.

