    android:id="@+id/myWebView"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent" />
```

2. In your Java code, find the WebView element by its ID and load a web page:

```java
WebView webView = findViewById(R.id.myWebView);
webView.loadUrl("https://www.example.com");
```

You can also customize the WebView behavior by setting a WebChromeClient or a WebViewClient. For example, to handle clicking on links within the WebView:

```java
webView.setWebViewClient(new WebViewClient() {
    @Override
    public boolean shouldOverrideUrlLoading(WebView view, String url) {
        // Open links within the WebView
        view.loadUrl(url);
        return true;
    }
});
```

And to handle other events, such as showing a progress bar when the page is loading:

```java
webView.setWebChromeClient(new WebChromeClient() {
    @Override
    public void onProgressChanged(WebView view, int newProgress) {
        // Show a progress bar while the page is loading
        ProgressBar progressBar = findViewById(R.id.progressBar);
        if (newProgress < 100) {
            progressBar.setVisibility(View.VISIBLE);
            progressBar.setProgress(newProgress);
        } else {
            progressBar.setVisibility(View.GONE);
        }
    }
});
```

Note that in order to display web content in your app, you need to add the INTERNET permission to your AndroidManifest.xml file:

```xml
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
```

To create a simple proxy server in Node.js, you can make use of the 'http' module. The following code demonstrates how to create a proxy server and forward requests from the user to another server:

```javascript
const http = require('http');
const httpProxy = require('http-proxy');

const proxy = httpProxy.createProxyServer({});
const targetUrl = 'http://targetserver.com'; // Replace with the target server URL

const proxyServer = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  proxy.web(req, res, { target: targetUrl });
});

proxyServer.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Proxy server is listening on http://localhost:3000');
});
```

Save the above code into a file named `proxyServer.js`. In this example, we use the `http-proxy` library to easily create a proxy server. Install the library by running `npm install http-proxy` in the terminal.

To run the proxy server, execute `node proxyServer.js` in the terminal. The server will start listening on `http://localhost:3000`. Now, any request made to this server will be forwarded to the `targetUrl` server.

