To implement a class named `MagicSquare` that generates a magic square for an odd-sized grid as per the given algorithm in Java, you can use the following code. This class includes a constructor that constructs the magic square of order `n` (where `n` is an odd number) and a `toString` method to return a string representation of the square for easy printing.

```java
public class MagicSquare {
    private int[][] square;

    public MagicSquare(int n) {
        if (n % 2 == 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("n must be odd.");
        square = new int[n][n];
        generateMagicSquare(n);
    }

    private void generateMagicSquare(int n) {
        int number = 1;
        int row = n - 1, col = n / 2; // Start from the middle of the bottom row
        square[row][col] = number; // Place the first number

        while (number < n * n) {
            int nextRow = (row + 1) % n;
            int nextCol = (col + 1) % n;

            if (square[nextRow][nextCol] == 0) { // If the next position is empty
                row = nextRow;
                col = nextCol;
            } else { // If right and down is occupied or in the bottom right corner
                row = (row - 1 + n) % n;
                // Do not change column, just go straight up
            }
            square[row][col] = ++number;
        }
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int[] row : square) {
            for (int number : row) {
                sb.append(String.format("%2d ", number));
            }
            sb.append("\n");
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            MagicSquare magicSquare = new MagicSquare(5); // Example size n=5
            System.out.println(magicSquare);
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

In this program, the `MagicSquare` class generates a magic square based on the algorithm provided. The `generateMagicSquare` method implements the specified logic to fill the square. The `toString` method creates a string representation of the square, which is formatted for clear visibility. In the `main` method, an example magic square is created and printed to demonstrate how the class works. This code should compile and run given n is an odd number. If an even number is given, the program raises an `IllegalArgumentException` informing that n must be odd.

