In Java, you don't directly "change" user input. Instead, you read the input from the user, usually as a string, and then you can process, modify, or convert this string as needed. Here's a basic example using the `Scanner` class to read user input from the console and then modify it:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class ChangeUserInput {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in); // Create a Scanner object to read input

        System.out.println("Enter a message: ");
        String userInput = scanner.nextLine();  // Read user input as a string

        // Modify the user input
        String modifiedInput = userInput.trim().toUpperCase(); // Trim whitespace and convert to uppercase

        System.out.println("Modified input: " + modifiedInput); // Display the modified input

        scanner.close(); // Close the scanner when done to prevent resource leaks
    }
}
```

In this example, the program is doing two things to "change" the user input:

- `.trim()` removes any leading and trailing whitespace from the input.
- `.toUpperCase()` converts all characters in the string to their uppercase equivalents.

If by changing user input, you mean to validate and correct it while the user is typing, like in a graphical user interface (GUI), then you would need to use event listeners to intercept user keystrokes and modify them accordingly. That is more complex and typically done with GUI frameworks like Swing or JavaFX.

Here is an example using a Swing `JTextField` to transform user input to uppercase as they type:

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.event.KeyAdapter;
import java.awt.event.KeyEvent;

public class ChangeUserInputSwing {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        JFrame frame = new JFrame();
        JTextField textField = new JTextField(20);

        // Add key listener to the text field to intercept user input
        textField.addKeyListener(new KeyAdapter() {
            @Override
            public void keyTyped(KeyEvent e) {
                char inputChar = e.getKeyChar();
                if (Character.isLowerCase(inputChar)) {
                    e.setKeyChar(Character.toUpperCase(inputChar)); // Convert to uppercase
                }
            }
        });

        frame.add(textField);
        frame.pack();
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }
}
```

In this GUI example, a `KeyListener` is added to the text field that converts each lowercase letter typed by the user into its uppercase equivalent.

An abstract component in Vue is a base component that is not meant to be used directly in the application but can be extended or used as a mixin to create other components with some shared functionality or structure. Here is an example of how to create an abstract Vue component:

1. Create a new file for your abstract component, e.g. `AbstractComponent.vue`
2. In this file, use the `components` prop to define the abstract component. You can use a render function to determine the structure of your component, or use mixins or provide/inject statements to share functionality across components. Here's an example:

```html
<script>
  export default {
    name: 'AbstractComponent',

    props: {
      title: String
    },

    data() {
      return {
        created: true
      };
    },

    render(h) {
      return h('div', [
        h('h3', this.title),
        this.$slots.default
      ]);
    }
  }
</script>
```
In this example, the abstract component has a `title` prop and wraps its children with a `div` and an `h3` tag that displays the title.

3. Create a new file for your actual component that uses the abstract component as a base, e.g. `ConcreteComponent.vue`. In this example, we'll extend the abstract component:

```html
<template>
  <div>
    <p>This is a concrete component that extends the abstract component.</p>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
  import AbstractComponent from './AbstractComponent.vue';

  export default {
    name: 'ConcreteComponent',

    extends: AbstractComponent
  }
</script>
```
Here, we import the `AbstractComponent.vue` and set the `extends` attribute to `AbstractComponent`. This allows us to use the functionality and structure from the abstract component. The resulting `ConcreteComponent.vue` component will render the title prop in an `h3` tag, followed by a `div` with its own contents.

