In C#, null values can indicate the absence of a value for a particular variable. Properly managing null values is crucial for preventing `NullReferenceException` errors, which occur when you attempt to access members (like methods or properties) of an object that is null.

Here are some strategies to manage null values in C#:

1. Null checks:
Before using an object, check if it is null.

```csharp
if (myObject != null)
{
    myObject.DoSomething();
}
```

2. Use the null-conditional operators:
The `?.` operator allows you to safely access members of an object only when it's not null. If the object is null, the operation returns null instead of throwing an exception.

```csharp
myObject?.DoSomething(); // This will do nothing if myObject is null
```

3. Use the null-coalescing operator `??`:
This operator provides a default value when the variable is null.

```csharp
string message = possiblyNullString ?? "Default message if null";
```

4. Use the null-coalescing assignment operator `??=` (introduced in C# 8.0):
Assigns the right-hand operand to the left-hand operand only if the left-hand operand evaluates to null.

```csharp
possiblyNullString ??= "Default message if null";
```

5. Avoid null where possible:
Make use of non-nullable reference types (introduced in C# 8.0). With nullable reference types enabled, the compiler will warn you when you might be dereferencing a null reference.

```csharp
#nullable enable
string nonNullableString = null; // Warning: Cannot convert null literal to non-nullable reference type
```

6. Use default values or the Null Object pattern:
Instead of allowing nulls, provide a default value or an object instance that behaves as a benign null (doesn't do anything).

```csharp
public class NullLogger : ILogger
{
    public void Log(string message)
    {
        // Do nothing
    }
}

ILogger logger = GetLogger() ?? new NullLogger();
logger.Log("Message"); // Safe, no matter what GetLogger returns
```

7. Use `Try` pattern:
When converting values or parsing, use the `Try` pattern which returns a Boolean indicating success and outputs the result into an out parameter.

```csharp
bool success = int.TryParse("123", out int result);
if (success)
{
    // Use result
}
```

8. Defensive programming:
Always assume that null values could be passed to your methods, constructors, or setters. Add null guard checks when necessary.

```csharp
public void DoSomethingWithObject(MyObject obj)
{
    if (obj == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(obj));
    // Rest of the method code...
}
```

Remember that with nullable reference types introduced in C# 8.0, you can explicitly annotate your code to distinguish between nullable and non-nullable reference types, making it easier for you to work with nulls more intentionally. This can be enabled with `#nullable enable` and becomes part of your static code analysis in your development environment.

