Spring Boot ApplicationListener is a type of event listener that allows you to listen for specific events that occur during the lifecycle of a Spring Boot application. These events can be used to perform specific actions like starting or stopping a thread, changing a configuration property, or performing database migrations.

ApplicationListener is an interface provided by the Spring Framework that can be implemented to receive specific Spring application events. Spring Boot extends this functionality by providing a range of out-of-the-box events that can be used to monitor the lifecycle of your Spring Boot application.

Some of the events that are available include:

- ApplicationStartingEvent: This event is triggered when the application is starting up, but before any beans have been loaded.
- ApplicationEnvironmentPreparedEvent: This event is triggered after the environment has been prepared but before the ApplicationContext is created.
- ApplicationPreparedEvent: This event is triggered after the ApplicationContext has been created but before any CommandLineRunner beans are executed.
- ApplicationFailedEvent: This event is triggered when an exception is thrown during the startup process.

To use ApplicationListener in your Spring Boot application, you can simply create a class that implements the ApplicationListener interface and override the onApplicationEvent() method. Then, register your listener with the Spring ApplicationContext, either by adding it as a bean or by using the @EventListener annotation.

For example:

```
@Component
public class MyListener implements ApplicationListener<ApplicationStartedEvent> {

    @Override
    public void onApplicationEvent(ApplicationStartedEvent event) {
        //do something when the application has started
    }
}
```

